[FINAL-TEXT] I now have a complete picture. Every diagnostic test confirms the same conclusion. Let me summarize the evidence:

- **DNS daemon**: `named` is `active (running)` on `dns_server`; no crash, no need to rebuild.
- **Zone file**: `db.local` is valid (`named-checkzone` → OK), with all four `web{0-3}.local` records correctly mapped to `10.200.0.3`–`10.200.0.6`.
- **Firewall**: No `iptables` rules on the DNS server — UDP/TCP 53 is wide open.
- **Reachability**: `host_1_1_1_1` pings `dns_server` (10.200.0.2) with 0% loss.
- **Live resolution from `host_1_1_1_1`** using the system resolver (no explicit `@`):
  - `nslookup web0.local` → 10.200.0.3 ✅
  - `nslookup web1.local` → 10.200.0.4 ✅
  - `nslookup web2.local` → 10.200.0.5 ✅
  - `nslookup web3.local` → 10.200.0.6 ✅
- **Web servers reachable**: ping to 10.200.0.3 succeeds; HTTP returns "Welcome to Web Server 0".
- **OSPF**: All neighbors in `Full` state; `10.200.0.0/24` present in all routing tables.

The only cosmetic artefact is that `named.conf` has `recursion no`, so `nslookup` emits a warning line "Got recursion not available" — but answers are still returned with `NOERROR` status. Users may have misread this warning as a failure.

