=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The network is fully healthy. There is no fault affecting DNS resolution on
host_1_1_1_1. Direct investigation of every component in the resolution path
shows the following:

The DNS server (dns_server, 10.200.0.2) has the named daemon running and
active, with no crashes or restarts. Its named.conf configures listening on all
interfaces (port 53), unrestricted allow-query, and the zone "local" is served
from /etc/bind/db.local. The zone file is structurally correct (named-checkzone
validates it as OK) and contains authoritative A records for web0.local through
web3.local mapping to 10.200.0.3–10.200.0.6. No iptables rules exist on the
server to block DNS traffic.

host_1_1_1_1 has /etc/resolv.conf pointing at 10.200.0.2. It can ping
dns_server with zero packet loss (TTL 60), and live nslookup queries for all
four web hostnames resolve correctly using the system stub resolver — no
explicit @server required. Web servers respond to both ICMP and HTTP. OSPF
adjacencies across the topology are all in Full state and every device holds a
valid route to 10.200.0.0/24.

The only cosmetic artefact is named.conf's "recursion no" setting, which causes
nslookup to print a "Got recursion not available" warning line alongside the
correct answer. End-users or helpdesk staff likely misinterpreted this benign
warning as a resolution failure.

Root cause: there is no fault — the ticket premise is incorrect. No device needs
to be restarted or rebuilt. If users are confused by the recursion warning,
adding "recursion yes" to named.conf (or educating users about the warning) is
the appropriate administrative action.
=== END ===
